  "title": "Start Chrome for Testing by default without its infobar; capture screenshots without a clip",
  "user": { "login": "konard", "id": 1431904, "node_id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=", "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1431904?v=4", "gravatar_id": "", "url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard", "html_url": "https://github.com/konard", "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/followers", "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/following{/other_user}", "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/gists{/gist_id}", "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/starred{/owner}{/repo}", "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/subscriptions", "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/orgs", "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/repos", "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/events{/privacy}", "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/konard/received_events", "type": "User", "user_view_type": "public", "site_admin": false },
  "body": "## Summary\n\nThis PR fixes both open issues, #179 and #180, in all three packages (JavaScript, Python and Rust). It also covers their parent issues, #181 and #183.\n\nFixes #183\nFixes #179\nFixes #180\nFixes #181\n\nPR #182 was an earlier attempt at #181. This PR includes all of that scope, so #182 is superseded.\n\n## #179: Chrome for Testing by default, with no infobar and no keychain prompt\n\n### Requirements and how each is met\n\n| Requirement from #179 | Implementation | Tests |\n|---|---|---|\n| A default restriction `no-chrome-for-testing-infobar` (`--test-type=gpu`) whenever the executable is Chrome for Testing | New restriction `no-chrome-for-testing-infobar` in the shared `launch-restrictions.json`, plus the preset `\"chrome-for-testing\": [\"no-chrome-for-testing-infobar\", \"mock-keychain\"]`. A launch whose executable is Chrome for Testing gets the preset before the caller's own restrictions. Any other browser gets nothing. `defaultRestrictions` / `default_restrictions` replaces the preset, and an empty list opts out. Launch results report the applied `defaultRestrictions`. | JS `launch-executable`, `launcher`, `real-browser`, `webdriver`, `parity` tests; Python `test_launch_executable.py`, `test_launch_browser.py`; Rust `chrome_for_testing_tests.rs`, `launcher_tests.rs`, `real_browser_tests.rs` |\n| `resolveLaunchExecutable()` defaults to Chrome for Testing, not the user's Google Chrome | With no `channel` and no `executablePath`, `launchBrowser()`, `launchRealBrowser()` and the WebDriver launch start Chrome for Testing. They start the build the engine itself reports first. If that build is missing, they take the newest build in the engine's download caches: Playwright (`PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH`), Puppeteer (`PUPPETEER_CACHE_DIR`), and for Selenium and the Rust engines also Selenium Manager's (`SE_CACHE_PATH`). Only then does Selenium ask Selenium Manager (`--browser chrome --force-browser-download`). The cache scan is the same in all three languages: `chrome-for-testing-cache.js`, `chrome_for_testing_cache.py` and `chrome_for_testing.rs`. When none is installed, the launch fails with an error that names the install command and the option `channel: 'chrome'`. Google Chrome starts only when asked for with `channel: 'chrome'` (or `msedge`, `brave`, `chromium`, or an explicit path). | Same tests as above, plus resolution-order, error-text and cache-scan tests (JS `chrome-for-testing-cache.test.js`, Python `test_chrome_for_testing_cache.py`, Rust `chrome_for_testing_tests.rs`) |\n| `--use-mock-keychain` by default for Chrome for Testing | Restriction `mock-keychain` in the same preset | Same tests |\n| Tests for each | Unit tests in all three languages. The new feature `launch.chrome-for-testing` in `docs/feature-parity/features.json` is claimed by tests in JS, Python and Rust, and the matrix is regenerated. | `node scripts/generate-feature-parity.mjs --check` |\n| Logins from the user's Google Chrome (from the issue's \"Root cause and fix\") | Documented in all three READMEs. Because of the mock keychain, Chrome for Testing cannot decrypt cookies in a copied Google Chrome profile. Move logins with `readBrowserCookies()` / `read_browser_cookies()` or `storageState`, which set the cookies through the browser's cookie API. | README sections |\n\nA path is detected as Chrome for Testing by `isChromeForTesting()` / `is_chrome_for_testing()`. It matches the archive directories (`chrome-linux64`, `chrome-win64`, `chrome-win32`, `chrome-mac-x64`, `chrome-mac-arm64`), the \"Google Chrome for Testing\" app name, and the Selenium Manager cache `selenium/chrome/<platform>/`.\n\n### API changes and migration\n\n- A launch without `channel` or an executable path now starts Chrome for Testing instead of Google Chrome. To keep the old behaviour, pass `channel: 'chrome'` (JS), `channel=\"chrome\"` (Python) or `.channel(\"chrome\")` (Rust).\n- Rust: `RealBrowserOptions::channel` is now `Option<String>`, where `None` means Chrome for Testing. The new `browser_channel()` returns the browser family, which is `\"chrome\"` when no channel is named.\n- New exports in every language:\n  - `isChromeForTesting` / `is_chrome_for_testing`\n  - `defaultRestrictionsFor` / `default_restrictions_for`\n  - `chromeForTestingRestrictions` / `chrome_for_testing_restrictions`\n- Snapshot launches and cookie-session launches still name their source browser, so they still read the user's real profile.\n- The e2e suites name `CHROME_PATH` or a channel, because CI installs Google Chrome and not Chrome for Testing. This covers `startup-readiness.e2e` on macOS and Windows and the issue #174 hand-off supervisor.\n\n### Evidence\n\n`experiments/issue-179/cft-infobar-probe.mjs` measures the height of the browser chrome (`outerHeight - innerHeight`) for several command lines. Only `--test-type=gpu` removes the infobar. `--disable-infobars`, `--test-type` and `--test-type=webdriver` do not, which matches `infobar_utils.cc`.\n\n`rust/examples/chrome_for_testing_default.rs` (run headful under Xvfb) launches with the defaults:\n\n```\nexecutable: ~/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-1248/chrome-linux64/chrome\nchrome for testing: true\ndefault restrictions: [\"no-chrome-for-testing-infobar\", \"mock-keychain\"]\ntest-type=gpu passed: true\nbrowser chrome height: Some(87)px\n\n# with --keep-infobar (default_restrictions = [])\ndefault restrictions: []\ntest-type=gpu passed: false\nbrowser chrome height: Some(143)px    <- the 56px infobar is back\n```\n\n`experiments/issue-179/selenium-manager-cft.mjs` shows that a default Selenium launch resolves to Selenium Manager's Chrome for Testing even when Google Chrome is installed.\n\n## #180: screenshots without a clip for traces and default captures\n\n### Cause\n\nFor a viewport screenshot, Playwright's `page.screenshot()` sends `Page.captureScreenshot` with a `clip` set to the viewport. If a headful window is resized during that capture, the page can keep the emulated size.\n\n### Fix\n\nTwo kinds of capture now use CDP `Page.captureScreenshot` with `{format, fromSurface: false, captureBeyondViewport: false}` and no `clip`:\n\n- trace checkpoint screenshots;\n- default screenshots of pages without a fixed viewport.\n\nThis applies in JS (`traces/recorder.js`, `capture/screenshot.js`), Python (`traces/engine.py`, `capture.py`) and Rust (`traces/page.rs`, `capture.rs`). Captures keep the engine's own screenshot in these cases:\n\n- the page has a fixed viewport;\n- the capture is a region, full-page or styled screenshot;\n- no CDP session is available;\n- the CDP capture fails.\n\n### Reproduction\n\n`experiments/issue-180/resize-during-screenshot.mjs`, with the results in `experiments/issue-180/results.md`. Each run makes 8 drags of 20 `Browser.setWindowBounds` steps while captures run in a loop, then compares `innerWidth` with `outerWidth`.\n\n| Capture | Stuck drags before | Stuck drags after |\n|---|---|---|\n| Playwright `page.screenshot({ type: 'png' })` | 3 of 8 | 3 of 8 (engine API, unchanged) |\n| `startTrace` checkpoints | 1 of 8 | **0 of 8** |\n\nWith `DEBUG=pw:protocol`, the checkpoints now send no `clip`.\n\n### Tests\n\n- JS `tests/unit/traces/issue-180-screenshots.test.js`\n- Python `tests/unit/traces/test_issue_180_screenshots.py`\n- Rust: `capture.rs` (`default_captures_take_the_view_without_a_clip`, …) and `traces/page.rs` (`checkpoint_screenshots_take_the_view_without_a_clip`)\n\n## Dependency advisories\n\nNew advisories published after main's last Security run made the audit jobs fail on any branch. This PR updates the lock files:\n\n- `js/package-lock.json`, dev-only:\n  - `launch-editor` 2.14.1 → 2.14.2 (GHSA-698v-4r3x-3vcv, GHSA-r825-w8mm-5pxw, GHSA-587h-7wmj-h5pm)\n  - `markdown-it` 14.3.1 → 14.3.3 (GHSA-r7fv-28h4-cvq7, GHSA-vfgh-jcmw-g37q, GHSA-gfr2-rv44-r23c)\n- `rust/Cargo.lock`: `rustls-webpki` 0.103.15 → 0.103.16 (RUSTSEC-2026-0382)\n\n## Release notes\n\n- JS changeset `js/.changeset/chrome-for-testing-default.md`\n- Python `changelog.d/179.changed.md` and `changelog.d/180.fixed.md`\n- Rust `changelog.d/20261021_160000_chrome_for_testing_default.md` (minor) and `changelog.d/20261021_160500_clipless_screenshots.md` (patch)\n\n## Local verification\n\n- **JS:** `npm test` (1935/1935), `startup-readiness.e2e` with `RUN_E2E=true` and no `CHROME_PATH`, `npm run lint`, `npm run format:check`, `npm run check:duplication`\n- **Python:**\n  - unit tests: 1330 passed, 2 skipped\n  - e2e with `RUN_E2E=true` under Xvfb: 16/16\n  - `ruff check`, `ruff format --check`, `mypy src`, `check_file_size.py`\n- **Rust:**\n  - `cargo check --locked --all-features --all-targets` with the updated lock\n  - `cargo fmt --check` and `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -D warnings`\n  - `cargo test --all-features`: all pass, 704 in the library\n  - the `--ignored` e2e suites that CI runs: `storage_state_smoke`, `reusable_session`, `snapshot`, `parity`, `webdriver`, `playwright_driver`, `puppeteer_bridge`, `trace_record_real_browser`, `launch_smoke`, `connect_smoke` and `real_browser_smoke`, all passing\n- **Repo:**\n  - `generate-feature-parity.mjs --check`\n  - `check-file-line-limits.sh`\n  - `check-shared-fingerprint-assets.sh`\n  - `rust/scripts/check-file-size.mjs`\n",
